Some researchers looked to the sky. A nearby star exploding as a [[supernova|supernova]] might have blasted Earth with harmful rays. Scientists looked for the chemical traces such a blast would leave and did not find them.

Others suggested a slow change in [[climate|climate]], with seas draining off the continents and temperatures swinging. Climate did change at the end of the age of dinosaurs. But a slow change struggles to explain why so many kinds of animals, on land and in the sea, vanished so quickly.

{{story:iridium-evidence}}

Scientists use a trick called the "living bracket". If birds and crocodiles both have a feature, their shared ancestors, including dinosaurs, probably had it too.

[[@croc-ancient|Crocodiles]] and alligators make deep bellows with their mouths closed. Male alligators can make the water dance on their backs with sounds partly too low for us to hear. Many birds, like pigeons, emus and ostriches, also call with closed mouths, cooing or booming. In 2016, a study showed that closed-mouth calling [[evolve|evolved]] several times in this family. So it is a good guess for big dinosaurs, but not a certainty.

{{story:elephants-talk}}

In 1971, a Polish and Mongolian team found one of the most famous fossils in the world. A Velociraptor is locked in battle with a Protoceratops, a plant-eater about the size of a sheep. The raptor's foot claw is in the Protoceratops's neck. The Protoceratops has the raptor's arm in its beak.

Both died together, probably when a sand dune collapsed on them. It is a frozen moment from 70-odd million years ago, and it shows that Velociraptor really did attack animals bigger than itself. Protoceratops was an early relative of [[@triceratops-horns|Triceratops]].

{{story:special-effects}}

Pangaea was shaped a bit like a giant letter C, wrapped around a sea called Tethys. Most of North America, Europe and Asia sat in the north. South America, Africa, Antarctica, Australia and India were packed together in the south.

With so much land in one lump, the middle was far from the ocean. Moist sea air rarely reached it, so huge areas were hot, dry desert, much larger than any of the [[@deserts-world|deserts we have today]]. Near the coasts, the [[climate|climate]] was wetter, with forests of ferns and early conifers.

{{story:great-dying}}

In the 1780s, the Italian scientist Luigi Galvani noticed that a dead frog's leg twitched when touched by two different metals. He thought the [[@electricity-made|electricity]] came from the frog. Another Italian scientist, Alessandro Volta, disagreed. Volta suspected the electricity came from the two metals, with the wet frog only connecting them.

To prove it, in 1800 Volta stacked discs of zinc and copper, with cardboard soaked in salty water between each pair. Touching the top and bottom of his "pile" gave a steady electric current, the first ever made without [[@lightning|lightning]] or rubbing. Scientists across Europe rushed to build their own. The volt, the unit that measures a battery's push, is named after him.

{{story:batteries-history}}

Every pitch falls because of [[@gravity-fall|gravity]]. Spin changes how much. Backspin on a fastball makes the air push upward, so it falls less. Topspin on a curveball pushes downward, so it falls more.

The air matters too. In thin mountain air, such as at the baseball stadium in Denver, Colorado, about 1.6 km above sea level, curveballs break less, because there is less air to push them. The same physics that lifts an [[@airplanes-fly|aeroplane]] wing is at work on a ball the size of an orange.

{{story:cricket-spin}}

For a ball thrown from the ground with no air, 45 degrees gives the longest throw. A javelin is different. It's released from about two metres up, above the thrower's head, which favours a lower angle. It also glides, and a flatter flight uses that better.

Most elite throws leave the hand at about 32 to 36 degrees. The tip should point almost the same way the javelin is moving. If the nose points too high, air pushes it up and it stalls, dropping short.

{{story:airplanes-fly}}

टक्कर के कुछ ही देर बाद तुम्हारा [[@immune-system|रोग-प्रतिरोधक तंत्र]] वहाँ मैक्रोफ़ेज नाम की सफ़ाई करने वाली कोशिकाएँ भेजता है। इनके नाम का अर्थ है “बड़े खाऊ”। ये फैली हुई लाल रक्त कोशिकाओं को निगल जाती हैं और हीमोग्लोबिन को तोड़ देती हैं।

पहले ये उसमें से लोहा अलग करती हैं, जिसे शरीर सँभालकर फिर इस्तेमाल करता है। जो बचता है, वह बिलिवर्डिन में बदल जाता है, जो हरा होता है। फिर बिलिवर्डिन बिलीरुबिन बन जाता है, जो पीला होता है। बिलीरुबिन खून के साथ [[@liver|तुम्हारे जिगर]] तक जाता है, जो उसे पित्त के साथ बाहर निकाल देता है।

{{story:colour-mix}}

In 2023, AI tutors began to appear in some classrooms. They are chatbots trained not to give answers straight away but to ask questions and offer hints, as a good tutor would. They can explain the same idea five different ways without losing patience.

They also make mistakes. An [[@ai-what|AI]] can state something wrong with total confidence. So experts say students need to learn how to check what AI tells them, and teachers need to guide how it's used. Some countries have tried AI textbooks and then pulled back after complaints from teachers and parents.

{{story:ai-what}}

The Chalukyas' great enemies were the [[@pallavas|Pallavas]] of Kanchipuram, in Tamil Nadu. Pulakeshin II attacked them, but in 642 the Pallava king Narasimhavarman I struck back, captured Vatapi, and Pulakeshin died.

The Chalukyas recovered after a few years. About a century later, King Vikramaditya II took revenge by capturing Kanchipuram. Instead of destroying it, he admired the Kailasanatha temple there and gave gifts to it. Pallava ideas then flowed north into Chalukya building.

{{story:rocks-types}}

सिंधु के कारीगर इंद्रगोप के मनकों के लिए मशहूर थे। इंद्रगोप एक लाल-नारंगी पत्थर है। वे रंग गहरा करने के लिए पत्थर को तपाते, फिर ख़ास पत्थर के बरमों से उसमें लंबे, पतले छेद करते। कुछ लंबे मनकों में कई दिनों का धीरज भरा काम लगता था।

उर की शाही क़ब्रों में ऐसे इंद्रगोप मनके मिले हैं जिन पर लाल पत्थर में सफ़ेद नक्काशी जलाकर बनाई गई है। [[archaeologist|पुरातत्वविद]] मानते हैं कि इनमें से बहुत से सिंधु में बने, या विदेश में बसे सिंधु के कारीगरों ने बनाए। सीप की चूड़ियाँ, हाथीदाँत और इमारती लकड़ी भी शायद पश्चिम की ओर जाती थीं। बदले में [[@indus-cities|सिंधु के शहरों]] को क्या मिला, यह पता लगाना ज़्यादा मुश्किल है, पर टिन और चाँदी जैसी धातुएँ शायद इसमें शामिल थीं।

{{story:trade-routes}}

Amsterdam began as a [[@people-fishing-villages|fishing village]] by a dam on the river Amstel, which gave the city its name. In the 1600s, during what the Dutch call their Golden Age, it became one of the richest trading cities in the world. Planners dug a ring of new canals in half-circles around the old centre, with tall merchants' houses along them.

Today Amsterdam has about 100 kilometres of canals and well over a thousand bridges. It is sometimes called the Venice of the North, though [[@venice|Venice]] was built on islands in a [[lagoon|lagoon]] rather than on drained marsh. In 2010, the canal ring became a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The house where [[@anne-frank|Anne Frank]] hid during the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]] stands beside one of these canals, the Prinsengracht.

{{story:anne-frank}}

During the last [[@ice-ages|ice age]], a sheet of ice more than a kilometre thick covered this part of North America. It flowed slowly south, scraping soft rock out of old river valleys and making them deeper and wider.

When the [[climate|climate]] warmed, the ice melted back. Meltwater filled the scooped-out basins, and the lakes took roughly their modern shape between about 14,000 and 10,000 years ago. The land is still slowly rising in places, springing back now that the weight of the ice has gone.

{{story:glaciers}}

Some cultures build names from parents' first names. In Iceland, a man named Jón whose father is Einar becomes Jón Einarsson, "Einar's son", and his sister might be Guðrún Einarsdóttir, "Einar's daughter". The Icelandic phone book lists people by first name.

In Tamil Nadu, many people put their father's first name as an initial before their own name, as in R. Lakshmi. The mathematician Srinivasa [[@ramanujan|Ramanujan]] carried his father's name, Srinivasa, in front of his own. In [[@russia|Russia]], a middle name comes from your father, so Ivan's son Pyotr is Pyotr Ivanovich.

{{story:families-world}}

After school, Bond went to Britain, hoping to become a writer. He took office jobs to pay the rent, and he missed India badly. Out of that homesickness came The Room on the Roof, written when he was about seventeen.

It tells of Rusty, an Anglo-Indian teenager who escapes his strict guardian and finds friends among Indian boys in Dehradun. It was published in 1956 and won the John Llewellyn Rhys Prize, a British award for writers under 35. With money from the book, Bond came home to India, and he has stayed.

{{story:monsoon}}

पिज़्ज़ा अक्सर 8 टुकड़ों में काटा जाता है, जिससे 2, 4 या 8 लोगों में बाँटना आसान होता है। 3 लोगों में बाँटना मुश्किल है, क्योंकि गोले की तिहाई करने के लिए बहुत सोच-समझकर काटना पड़ता है। इसीलिए टीचर [[@fractions|भिन्न]] समझाने के लिए पिज़्ज़ा का सहारा लेना पसंद करते हैं। हर टुकड़ा पूरे का एक [[fraction|भिन्नात्मक हिस्सा]] होता है।

{{story:fractions}}

अमेरिका के कुछ शहरों में चौकोर पिज़्ज़ा चलते हैं, जिन्हें तिकोनी फाँकों की जगह छोटे-छोटे चौकोर टुकड़ों में काटा जाता है। गणितज्ञों ने पिज़्ज़ा काटने के सबसे न्यायपूर्ण तरीक़ों पर शोध-पत्र तक लिखे हैं, और साबित किया है कि कुछ चतुर तरीक़ों से काटने पर सबको बिल्कुल बराबर हिस्सा मिलता है।

Soybeans are a kind of [[legume|legume]], like peas, [[@lentils-world|lentils]] and chickpeas. Their roots have small bumps called nodules, where [[bacteria|bacteria]] live. These bacteria take nitrogen gas from the air and turn it into a form the plant can use.

That means soybeans need less [[fertiliser|fertiliser]] than many crops, and they can leave the [[@soil-made|soil]] a little richer for the next crop. Farmers in the American Midwest often grow soybeans one year and [[@corn-maize|maize]] the next.

{{story:brazil-amazon}}

A good chew stick is more than a scrubber. [[@neem|Neem]] twigs are bitter, and neem contains substances that may slow the growth of some mouth [[bacteria|bacteria]]. Miswak sticks have natural fibres and a little fluoride.

Chewing the end of the stick makes a brush that reaches between teeth. In many [[@villages-india|Indian villages]], people still start the day with a fresh datun. Studies suggest chew sticks can clean teeth fairly well when used properly, though dentists say a toothbrush with fluoride toothpaste is more reliable.

{{story:neem}}
